.TH YOM-HOOKS 1 "2026-07-10" "v2.2.0-part1" "YOM User Manual"
.SH NAME
yom-hooks \- lightweight hook API for the YOM shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hook
.I HOOKED PATH
.SH DESCRIPTION
The YOM-hook API allows you to change 
.I YOM's 
builtins to run a binary, or basically any other action, the following created
hooks are:
.I builtins cd core echo eval exit pwd read

.SH HOW TO USE
To use hooks, you just have to write this in a file then execute with YOM:
.RS
.EX
hook <HOOKED> <PATH/TO/HOOK>
.EE
.RE
.PP
for all the builtins, you'd do:
.RS
.EX
hook builtins <PATH/TO/HOOK>
.EE
.RE
the following accepted hooks are in the list above. 
.I <PATH/TO/HOOK>
is where the hook is located, this can go from the home directory, specified
by a tilde, or the root directory; specified by a slash.
.PP
To disable a hook, simply do:
.RS
.EX
hook <HOOKED> default
.EE
.RE
and it will change it to default

.SS BUILTINS
.B yom 
has hooks for all builtins provided, that being: \fIbuiltins cd echo exit pwd read\fR,
.I builtins 
is a shortcut to do all the 
.B builtins

.SS CORE & EVAL
.I core
should be used when you want to add an interactivity layer, the calling is the 
same as the others, just:
.RS
.EX
hook core <PATH/TO/HOOK>
.EE
.RE
.B eval
follows the same thing, just:
.RS
.EX
hook eval <PATH/TO/HOOK>
.EE
.RE
what
.B eval
is for is to add new commands by evaluating the input string and then running it
or exiting

.SH API

.SS CD
The
.B cd
hook gives you the directory as
.I arg1
and stdout is piped, simply dump to
.I stdout
the full path of the directory to cd to.

.SS CORE
The most powerful hook on this list, 
.B core

.SS ECHO
The
.B echo
hook simply works like this:
.I arg1
is just the string to echo, it gives you full access to
.I stdout stderr
and
.I stdin

.SS READ

